  "query_preview": "..\n\nAn upper level high currently centered over central Arizona will\ngradually lift north into the Four Corners through the week.\nThe high's current position and orientation has allowed for\nactive easterly flow to set up over southeastern Arizona, while\nsurface dew points have increased to the upper 50s to lower 60s\nacross the area. Under modestly divergent flow aloft,\nthunderstorms are expected to initiate over the Sky Islands and\nWhite Mountains early to mid afternoon today, with the easterly\nflow moving and expanding their coverage westward through the\nevening. 1000-2000 J/kg of MLCAPE and 1.2-1.6 inches of\nprecipitable water will provide ample instability, combining\nwith 15-25 knots of effective shear for a severe, organized\nthunderstorm threat. While most of southeastern Arizona has at\nleast a 30-50 percent chance of thunderstorms today, coverage \nshould be greatest through southern Cochise, Santa Cruz, and \ninto south central Pima counties. It's in this area, especially \nSanta Cruz, where potential for multiple storm cells and \nsubsequent flash flooding is greater. A Flood Watch remains in \neffect this afternoon and evening. \n\nElsewhere today a scattered severe wind threat remains, \nespecially where the 12Z HREF highlights the stronger potential \nfor organized severe outflows transitioning south off the White \nMountains this afternoon and this evening further west if \nthunderstorm outflows organize west of Tucson. These winds will \nlikely produce a blowing dust threat, especially in dust prone \nareas of I-10 east of Willcox and west of Tucson into Pinal and \nthrough central to western Pima. A Blowing Dust Advisory remains\nin place for this potential, with the possibility that warnings\nwill be needed where organized outflows kick up areas of very \ndense blowing dust. Finally, an isolated severe hail threat \ncan't be ruled out with any sustained thunderstorm today. \n\nOn Tuesday the upper high nudges north slightly while keeping\neasterly flow in place over the region. Again scattered \nthunderstorms are likely, however location will depend on \ntoday's action. 12Z guidance points to a later start with an \nemphasis on eastern areas, mainly Graham, Greenlee, Cochise, \ninto Santa Cruz counties. Forecast soundings further west (such \nas at Tucson) show a modest warming inversion which would line \nup with the start in the east. A late evening push into \nPima/Pinal counties however can't be ruled out, depending on the\nlongevity and organization of outflows. The CAPE/shear \nparameter space will remain impressive for severe winds and \nblowing dust, along with an isolated flash flooding risk. \nAdditional blowing dust headlines for tomorrow in eastern\nlocations may be needed if this trend continues.\n\nFavorable flow for monsoon activity continues through the week\nwith the upper high drifting north towards the Four Corners\nwith day to day variability. Forecast high temperatures will \nfall to 2-4 degrees above normal, with some variability due to \nthunderstorm activity and cloud cover.",
